WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.849 --> 00:00:17.039
We are here in the basement of the Royal Society
in London, The Royal Society is the UK National

00:00:17.039 --> 00:00:22.180
Academy for Sciences and we are here because
there is an exhibition about Rutherford.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:27.300
We are interested in the element Rutherfordium
which is right here at the bottom of the periodic

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:35.180
table number 104, and with these elements
only tiny amounts, relatively few atoms have

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:40.309
been made so we can’t tell you about how
it is used in light bulbs or something like

00:00:40.309 --> 00:00:45.640
that because it has not been used for anything.
People are really excited if they make one

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:51.999
or two atoms of it. So instead we are going
to tell you a bit about Rutherford after whom

00:00:51.999 --> 00:00:58.379
it’s named. And Rutherford is famous because
he did a whole series of experiments, or he

00:00:58.379 --> 00:01:04.729
and his co-workers, that really explained
the structure of atoms and so the periodic

00:01:04.729 --> 00:01:11.920
table for the first time really began to make
sense. Over here you can see there is a picture

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:19.880
of Rutherford here in his lab with one of
his co-workers and this is a very famous picture.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:25.960
Rutherford, who came from New Zealand, had
a very loud voice and the experiments were

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:30.909
rather sensitive, if you talked too loud the
apparatus started shaking and it did not work

00:01:30.909 --> 00:01:38.060
properly, so his students put up a big notice
in the lab saying talk softly please. But

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:42.869
I am not sure that it stopped him and I use
this picture when I am talking to my students

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:47.649
about safety, you’ve always got to be really
safe when you are working as a chemist. First

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:53.200
of all you can see that Rutherford is smoking
in the lab and then he is not wearing safety

00:01:53.200 --> 00:02:00.049
glasses, nor is his colleague, and then they
have a whole series of wires and all sorts

00:02:00.049 --> 00:02:05.579
of things to trip over. So it is really surprising
that they managed to get their experiments

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:10.789
to work without some sort of serious accident.
Perhaps they did break things and never told

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:18.200
us about it. Anyway Rutherford’s work was
recognised by him winning the Nobel Prize

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:25.900
and here, and it is quite exciting, there
is a replica of the Nobel Prize. The Nobel

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:35.250
Prize is a gold medal but here they have shown
it as two half-medals, underneath there is

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:42.710
nothing to see, so you can see both of the
sides. So on one side there is the portrait

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:51.980
of Alfred Nobel, whose money and foundation,
still to this day, pays for prize money, when

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:57.900
he received this it would have been presented
to him by the Swedish King in a huge ceremony

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:07.480
in Stockholm. Now what I can’t remember
is whether he got the Nobel Prize for physics

00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:11.180
or chemistry. I have a feeling…

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.180
Chemistry!

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.950
Yes. I have a feeling that he got it for chemistry
which was particularly funny because Rutherford

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:24.420
didn’t like chemistry. He said that there
is just physics and stamp collecting and he

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:29.120
did not really respect chemistry so perhaps
the chemists got their revenge by giving him

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.260
the Nobel Prize for chemistry.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:37.780
Now Rutherford had very strong views about
the hours his students should work and they

00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:44.220
were not allowed to work after 6 o’clock
at night in the lab and his chief technician

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:50.000
would go round the lab at 6 o’clock and
switch off all the apparatus and chase everybody

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:59.100
out of the lab. And even though they did this,
11 of his students won Nobel Prizes; this

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:06.250
must be one of the records for any single
research group to this day. And of course

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:10.810
when they went home having had dinner and
drank wine in the college then they started

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:16.959
working writing papers. But it is very interesting
that if you read letters from visiting American

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:23.250
scientists they could not understand how Rutherford’s
lab was so successful because the people came

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:27.980
in at 10 o’clock in the morning they went
home at 6, and did not seem to do any work

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:30.620
at all, but they discovered all these wonderful
things.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:34.630
What’s your policy on your students working
then?

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:40.200
Well I always encourage my students to work
but I find that if I encourage them too hard,

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:46.391
it has the opposite effect, just like parents
telling children to do their homework but

00:04:46.391 --> 00:04:50.850
by and large when people are enthusiastic
about their science it is difficult to stop

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:52.510
them working too much.

00:04:52.510 --> 00:05:00.280
As part of this exhibition they have got some
postcards of Rutherford and this one has got,

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:04.930
I think it is really terrific, shows Rutherford
in the sea. But I really like this because

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:11.490
it shows that famous scientists sometimes
take time off and they don’t always behave

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:16.330
like white-coated figures in labs, but they
do have a human side as well.

